The best and most common way to change a `<span>` element's color on mouseover is using **CSS**. 

Here are the three different ways you can achieve this, ordered from the most recommended to the least.

---

### Method 1: Using CSS `:hover` (Recommended)
This is the cleanest, most performant, and standard way. You use the CSS `:hover` pseudo-class.

**HTML:**
```html
<span class="hover-text">Hover over me!</span>
```

**CSS:**
```css
/* The default color of the span */
.hover-text {
  color: black;
  transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Optional: makes the color change smooth */
}

/* The color of the span when hovered */
.hover-text:hover {
  color: red;
}
```

---

### Method 2: Inline JavaScript (Quickest for simple/single elements)
If you cannot edit a separate stylesheet and need a quick, inline solution, you can use the HTML `onmouseover` and `onmouseout` attributes.

```html
<span style="color: black; transition: color 0.3s;" 
      onmouseover="this.style.color='red'" 
      onmouseout="this.style.color='black'">
  Hover over me!
</span>
```

---

### Method 3: JavaScript `addEventListener` (Best for dynamic elements)
If you are writing JavaScript and want to keep your HTML clean while dynamically adding this effect to one or multiple spans:

**HTML:**
```html
<span id="my-span">Hover over me!</span>
```

**JavaScript:**
```javascript
const span = document.getElementById('my-span');

// Change color on mouse enter
span.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {
  span.style.color = 'red';
});

// Reset color on mouse leave
span.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
  span.style.color = 'black';
});
```

### Which one should you choose?
* Use **Method 1 (CSS)** in almost all cases. It separates your styling from your markup, is highly performant, and handles mobile touch states the best.

